################################################################################
# nanosoc_iice.fdc — Synplify FDC translation of fpga/rp/nanosoc/nanosoc_ooc.xdc
#
# WHY A SEPARATE FILE AT ALL
# --------------------------
# Synplify does NOT accept XDC. There is no `sdc2fdc` in this install either —
# $SYNPLIFY_HOME/bin ships only pdc2sdc and qsf2sdc — so this is a hand
# translation, kept line-for-line traceable to its XDC source. The reverse-
# direction precedent is
#   nanosoc-ethernet-chiplet/fpga/haps-sx/scripts/fdc_to_xdc.py
# and the dialect used here matches the working, in-use
#   nanosoc-ethernet-chiplet/fpga/haps-sx/constraints/nanosoc_eth_chiplet_haps_sx.fdc
# which uses the SDC-standard subset (create_clock / set_clock_groups /
# set_false_path / get_ports / get_clocks) rather than the legacy Synplify
# spellings (define_clock / define_false_path / {p:port}). Both are accepted;
# the SDC subset keeps this file diffable against the XDC.
#
# SCOPE. This constrains rp_nanosoc_iice_core — the Synplify/Identify synthesis
# top, whose port list is byte-identical to rp_nanosoc_wrapper's (the 35
# contract ports of docs/contracts/partition-pins.md). So every constraint
# below has exactly the same meaning it has in the Vivado baseline.
#
# WHAT IS NOT HERE, DELIBERATELY:
#   * identify_jtag_tck. The Identify instrumentor ADDS that port and brings its
#     own constraint with it — $SYNPLIFY_HOME/lib/share/synthesis/syn.sdc:
#       define_clock {identify_jtag_tck} -period 250.0 -clockgroup identify_jtag_group
#     (250 ns = 4 MHz). Declaring it here too would create two competing
#     definitions of the same clock. Note the soft TAP does NOT need a global
#     clock buffer because `device skewfree 1` builds master/slave flops on the
#     JTAG chain instead — mandatory, since the RP pblock has ZERO BUFG sites.
#   * pin locations / I/O standards. This is an out-of-context, pad-less
#     netlist (`set_option -disable_io_insertion 1`); the static shell owns
#     every pad. Nothing in this file may ever place a pin.
#
# UNMATCHED OBJECTS. rp_nanosoc_wrapper ties several boundary ports off inert
# (phy_rmii_*, mdio_*, swo, dut_lockup, irq_out), so a port can optimise to a
# constant and vanish. The XDC handles that with `get_ports -quiet`; Synplify
# has no -quiet, and reports an unmatched constraint object as a WARNING (@W),
# not an error. That is the intended behaviour here — a missing port is not a
# failure. Do not "fix" the warnings by deleting the constraints; they must
# stay so that a future wrapper which DOES drive those ports is still
# constrained.
#
# Target: Synplify Premier 2022.09-SP2, XCKU115 / FLVB1760 / -1-c.
################################################################################

################################################################################
# GLOBAL BUFFER INFERENCE — the RP pblock has ZERO BUFG sites, so this must be
# OFF for the whole design. Not an optimisation: an inferred global buffer
# anywhere inside the RM is unplaceable.
#
# MEASURED FAILURE, 2026-07-29, on the real 240-source DUT. `make synth` was
# clean and `make dcp` linked 21,094 cells, then the no-BUFG gate in
# ooc_synth_synplify.tcl fired on TWO cells:
#
#   u_rm/u_nanosoc/u_qspi_flash_0/u_top_ahb_qspi/u_qspi_clock_div/
#       partial_QSPI_SCLK_i_cb                      <- divided QSPI clock
#   u_rm/u_nanosoc/u_ss_cpu/u_cpu_0/u_core_prmu/u_rstctrl/
#       u_core_hresetn_sync/rst_sync2_n_buf         <- reset synchroniser
#
# Both are DESIGN nets. Nothing in the .idc is responsible — the .idc had both
# `device xilinxinsertbufg 0` and `device skewfree 1` (make synth refuses to run
# without them). This is Synplify's own automatic promotion of high-fanout
# clock AND reset nets onto global buffers; Vivado does not do it, which is why
# the fpga/rp/nanosoc/ (Vivado) baseline never hit this.
#
# THE CONTROL, confirmed in the installed documentation before use — no guessed
# flag names:
#
#   syn_noclockbuf   "Turns off automatic clock buffer usage."
#                    fpga_attribute_reference.pdf p.415-418:
#                      * Vendor Xilinx: technology "all"
#                      * Values: 0/false = ON (default), 1/true = OFF
#                      * "Global Support: Yes", Object: module/architecture
#                      * FDC syntax, verbatim from p.416:
#                            define_attribute {clock_port} syn_noclockbuf {0|1}
#                            define_global_attribute syn_noclockbuf {0|1}
#                    fpga_reference.pdf p.623 restates the scope: "Turns off
#                    automatic clock buffers for ENTIRE MODULES OR NETS, or just
#                    for specific input ports."
#
# `define_global_attribute` is a documented FDC command, not an SDC-only one:
# fpga_reference.pdf p.150 — "This FDC file includes both timing constraints and
# non-timing constraints in a single file ... Non-timing (or design constraints)
# ... include constraints such as define_attribute, define_io_standard".
#
# The GLOBAL form is used deliberately, in preference to naming the two nets:
# this RM can never host a BUFG at all, so "never infer one, anywhere" is the
# actual requirement, and a per-net list would silently miss the next net that
# crosses the fanout threshold when the DUT changes.
#
# Consistency check that this is the right lever: the Identify instrumentor
# itself emits `wire identify_clk /* syn_noclockbuf=1 */` on its own clock net
# when `device xilinxinsertbufg 0` is set. Same attribute, same purpose.
#
# Side effect, and it is the DESIRED one: dut_clk (the highest-fanout clock in
# the RM) also loses any buffer. Correct — dut_clk arrives as a partition pin
# ALREADY buffered by the static shell's clk_wiz_dut, and the Vivado baseline's
# util_rm_nanosoc.rpt section 8 (CLOCK) is an empty table, i.e. the baseline RM
# contains zero BUFGs too. Suppressing inference here makes the Synplify RM
# match the Vivado RM rather than diverging from it.
#
# Deliberately NOT used: `define_global_attribute syn_global_buffers {0}`.
# syn_global_buffers is real, Xilinx-supported and global
# (fpga_attribute_reference.pdf p.299-300: "prevent the synthesis tool from
# inferring clock buffers"), but the docs say only "for Xilinx designs, the
# value varies with the technology" and specify a floor of 6 for Microchip —
# a value of 0 is not documented as valid for Xilinx. syn_noclockbuf is
# unambiguous, so it is used alone; adding a second, semi-documented knob in the
# same run would have made the result unattributable.
#
# `set_option -globalthreshold` is NOT the answer either: it is Microchip-only
# (fpga_reference.pdf Appendix D, p.795).
#
# PER-NET FALLBACK, if the global attribute is ever insufficient. Note the FDC
# object syntax: `n:` prefix for a net and `.` (NOT `/`) as the hierarchy
# separator — the convention Synplify's own xilinx_builtin.sdc uses
# (`n:comm_block_INST.tck`). Note also that Vivado shows the buffer Synplify
# ADDED (`..._cb`, `..._buf`); the net to constrain is the one WITHOUT that
# suffix, and the `u_core/` prefix Vivado prints does not exist in Synplify's
# view (Synplify's top is rp_nanosoc_iice_core, so paths start at u_rm).
#
# define_attribute {n:u_rm.u_nanosoc.u_qspi_flash_0.u_top_ahb_qspi.u_qspi_clock_div.partial_QSPI_SCLK} syn_noclockbuf {1}
# define_attribute {n:u_rm.u_nanosoc.u_ss_cpu.u_cpu_0.u_core_prmu.u_rstctrl.u_core_hresetn_sync.rst_sync2_n} syn_noclockbuf {1}
################################################################################
define_global_attribute syn_noclockbuf {1}

################################################################################
# dut_clk — the DUT system clock (partition-pins.md "Clocks & resets").
# Carries the static's clk_out1_shell_bd_clk_wiz_dut_0 (DRP MMCM output).
# Period/waveform copied BYTE-FOR-BYTE from docs/contracts/partition-timing.md
# via nanosoc_ooc.xdc:40 — 20.000 ns / {0.000 10.000} / 50 MHz. If the shell
# retunes clk_wiz_dut, or firmware DRP-reprograms it, update partition-timing.md,
# nanosoc_ooc.xdc AND this line together.
#
# Everything real in this RM runs on dut_clk: the whole nanosoc core
# (sys_clk = dut_clk) and the uart_axis_shim (clk = dut_clk).
#
# The IICE's SAMPLE clock is this clock. Identify requires the sample clock to
# be a global clock resource sourced from a BUFG; dut_clk arrives as a partition
# pin already buffered by the static shell's clk_wiz_dut, so it IS on a global
# net — but the BUFG is OUTSIDE the RP. Whether Identify accepts that rather
# than demanding a local buffer is UNVERIFIED (plan §4 "Check"). If it inserts
# one anyway the build fails at Vivado placement, not here: there is no BUFG
# site in the pblock.
################################################################################
create_clock -name {dut_clk} -period 20.000 -waveform {0.000 10.000} \
    [get_ports {dut_clk}]

################################################################################
# jtag_tck — the DUT debug clock (fpga/shell/boundary.yaml group `jtag`), and
# in the INSTRUMENTED build ALSO the Identify soft TAP's TCK: the two TAPs are
# daisy-chained on this one wire-set, so they share TCK exactly (see
# rp_nanosoc_iice_shim.sv, docs/planning/IICE_JTAG_CHAIN.md).
#
# WAS `swd_clk` until the 2026-09-10 rebase. The swd_* group does not exist in
# the fielded boundary (mint 0xA8C1C535); the shell's swd_bb was replaced by
# jtag_bb @ 0x44A7_0000 at the A6 SWD->JTAG cutover.
#
# In the static this is a firmware bit-banged CSR pin (JTAGBB.DRIVE[0],
# shell-regmap.md), NOT a clk_wiz output — it has NO create_clock in the static
# and is DC-slow. 100 ns (10 MHz) is a generous upper bound on any real
# firmware bit-bang rate and is deliberately TIGHTER than Identify's own 250 ns
# on identify_jtag_tck, so this file can never be the reason a JTAG path is
# under-constrained. It is NOT a shell contract value.
#
# It ALSO covers the one path the chain creates that neither TAP owns alone:
# Identify's TDO (launched on a jtag_tck edge) into the SWJ-DP's TDI (captured
# on a jtag_tck edge). That splice lives in the SHIM, above this synthesis top,
# and is timed by nanosoc_ooc.xdc's identically-valued `create_clock -name
# jtag_tck -period 100.000` at checkpoint time. Both files must keep the same
# period or the chain path is analysed at two different rates in two flows.
#
# OOC-ONLY: this clock has no static counterpart, so it lives here and in no
# _rm.xdc (partition-timing.md O2). Mirrors nanosoc_ooc.xdc:107.
################################################################################
create_clock -name {jtag_tck} -period 100.000 [get_ports {jtag_tck}]

################################################################################
# SWJ-DP (jtag_tck) vs core (dut_clk) is an internal async crossing owned by the
# CoreSight DAP's own synchronizers. Declare it async so the OOC run does not
# time a false jtag_tck<->dut_clk path. From nanosoc_ooc.xdc:110-114.
#
# Synplify note: clocks in DIFFERENT -clockgroups are treated as asynchronous
# by default, so this set_clock_groups is belt-and-braces over the automatic
# behaviour rather than the only thing preventing a false path. Keeping it
# explicit means the FDC says the same thing the XDC says.
################################################################################
set_clock_groups -asynchronous \
    -group [get_clocks {dut_clk}] \
    -group [get_clocks {jtag_tck}]

################################################################################
# Async partition-pin DATA ports — false-pathed for standalone OOC analysis.
# partition-pins.md "Clock/reset domain rule": every boundary crossing is CDC'd
# on the STATIC side (uart_bridge async FIFOs, board_gpio synchronizers, the
# jtag_bb bit-banger, the VPHY). From this RM's view they arrive already-safe,
# so timing them against dut_clk standalone would model a crossing the static
# already owns. From nanosoc_ooc.xdc:117-149.
################################################################################
# Async control inputs: the three resets (async-assert, deassert synchronized
# shell-side; this RM does not re-sync).
#
# jtag_tdi/jtag_tms/jtag_tdo are NOT in this list and must not be: they are
# real 1149.1 serial legs, launched and captured on jtag_tck, and jtag_tck has
# a create_clock above. False-pathing them here would leave the SWJ-DP's own
# TAP shift registers unconstrained in the Synplify run — which is exactly the
# analysis this chain most needs, because the splice at the shim level makes
# jtag_tdi an INTERNAL chain hop rather than a shell-driven pin in the
# instrumented build.
#
# (nanosoc_ooc.xdc:133 DOES false-path jtag_tdi, at the Vivado/checkpoint level
# and for the SINGLE-TAP baseline, where the port really is fed only by the
# shell's bit-banger. That file is fpga/rp/nanosoc's, is applied additively
# after synth_design, and is deliberately not edited from here — the difference
# is recorded rather than papered over.)
set_false_path -from [get_ports {dut_resetn rp_resetn dbg_resetn}]

# Console AXIS byte stream (uart_bridge async FIFOs, static side) —
# host->DUT in, DUT->host out.
set_false_path -from [get_ports {uart_rx_tdata[*] uart_rx_tvalid uart_tx_tready}]
set_false_path -to   [get_ports {uart_tx_tdata[*] uart_tx_tvalid uart_rx_tready swo}]

# Board-GPIO passthrough (board_gpio 2-FF synchronizers, static side).
#
# The old `IICE_OWNS_SWD=0` note that used to sit here is GONE with the design
# it described: the shim no longer muxes TAP ownership onto a spare GPIO bit,
# because a daisy chain makes both TAPs reachable at once and there is nothing
# left to arbitrate. dut_gpio_i is now purely the DUT's board-port input.
set_false_path -from [get_ports {dut_gpio_i[*]}]
set_false_path -to   [get_ports {dut_gpio_o[*] dut_gpio_oe[*]}]

# Ethernet group — structurally tied off inert in this RM (no MAC), but
# false-path defensively in case synthesis keeps any port.
set_false_path -from [get_ports {phy_rmii_ref_clk phy_rmii_crs_dv phy_rmii_rxd[*] mdio_i}]
set_false_path -to   [get_ports {phy_rmii_txd[*] phy_rmii_tx_en mdc mdio_o mdio_oe}]

# Status / misc telemetry to the shell (rm_id is a constant; dut_lockup/irq_out
# tied off in this RM).
set_false_path -to   [get_ports {rm_id[*] dut_lockup irq_out}]

################################################################################
# NOT TRANSLATED from nanosoc_ooc.xdc — and why
#
# The 2026-10 ILA mint's dbgbscan block (2x create_clock dbg_bscan_tck/drck at
# 80 ns, 1x set_clock_groups async_dbg_bscan, 2x set_false_path on the unused
# BSCAN legs). The core passes those legs to rp_nanosoc_wrapper, which ignores
# them and ties dbg_bscan_tdo low, so Synplify sees no logic on them and needs
# no constraint. Everything else in nanosoc_ooc.xdc (2x create_clock, 1x
# set_clock_groups, 7x set_false_path) is above. The QSPI group
# (qspi_sclk/csn/io_o/io_oe/io_i) has NO constraint in the XDC either — it is
# the documented non-CDC source-synchronous exception (partition-pins.md v0.2)
# and is constrained on the STATIC side, so this file must not touch it.
################################################################################
